WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.879
The serving runtime turns model files and accelerator resources into a network service.

00:00:04.879 --> 00:00:10.011
Llama.cpp server is portable and effective for quantized models across CPU and several GPU

00:00:10.011 --> 00:00:14.160
backends. Ollama packages model management behind a straightforward local service.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.765
These tools are useful for labs and modest deployments where operational simplicity

00:00:17.765 --> 00:00:19.998
matters more than maximizing concurrent throughput.

00:00:19.998 --> 00:00:24.350
VLLM is designed for high-throughput language-model serving and includes memory-management

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.800
and batching techniques suited to concurrent requests on supported hardware.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.070
Text Generation Inference, often called TGI,

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:34.840
is another production-oriented server with model-serving and observability features.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.150
Their advantages appear only when models, accelerators,

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:41.475
and traffic patterns are compatible. Benchmark the actual model and prompt distribution;

00:00:41.475 --> 00:00:45.480
do not select a runtime solely from an impressive result produced on different hardware.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.508
An OpenAI-compatible API can decouple clients from the underlying server.

00:00:49.508 --> 00:00:53.543
Applications use a familiar request format while a gateway or router directs traffic to

00:00:53.543 --> 00:00:56.395
llama.cpp, Ollama, VLLM, or another backend.

00:00:56.395 --> 00:01:00.690
Compatibility is not always identical across parameters, streaming, tool calls,

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:04.552
embeddings, and error behavior, so test the features your clients depend on.

00:01:04.552 --> 00:01:06.840
Open WebUI can provide a shared browser front end,

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.200
but it should use the same authentication and routing controls as other clients.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.000
Introduce model routing only after one server is reliable.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.275
Start a known model, confirm acceleration, measure latency and throughput,

00:01:17.275 --> 00:01:20.030
exercise health checks, and document startup and shutdown.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:22.966
Then replicate the service or add another model role.

00:01:22.966 --> 00:01:25.760
Complex orchestration cannot repair an unstable serving process;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.400
it only distributes the instability.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:31.440
A simple, observable serving layer is the foundation for safe scheduling and scale.
